# THE GREET DRIVE: a new PR in example/app gets one comment saying "hi from verse",
# with a person approving each one.
#
# Two strata, two rules, the issue-triage pattern with a gate in it. A PR appears; a row is
# derived for it; an ASK gate holds the firing until somebody answers `verse gate approve`;
# then a task is spawned that NAMES `broker`, so it lands unclaimed and waits for a session
# with GitHub credentials to claim it (.claude/skills/verse-work, or by hand with
# `verse task do`). The row's `stage` is the latch: set on completion, so a PR is greeted once.
#
#     verse c -a -f docs/working/greet.tony
#
# INSTALLING BRIEFS THE BACKLOG, for issue-triage's reason: "new" is unsayable, so every open
# PR gets a row and a gate the moment this installs. To skip the backlog, install in two steps:
# apply the file with `greet-pr` removed, wait for the rows, mark them —
#
#     verse data verse-dev greet row --paths   # one row per open PR
#     verse data verse-dev greet row '<id>' --put '{stage: pre-existing}'
#
# — then re-apply the full file. Rows carrying a stage are outside `greet-pr`'s selection, so
# only PRs opened after that are greeted. Do NOT latch with a rule; that is the storm
# issue-triage.tony documents.
#
# The write credential is the point of `runner: broker`: versed has no GitHub write token and
# should not — the comment is posted from a claimed session where `gh` is already
# authenticated, and the claim is attributed (`--as`).
at: verse-dev.greet
reads: [ !verse-path [github] ]
---
# The reflected slice, where a PR is the subject. Level 0: the source writes here,
# this package never does.
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: github

# One row per PR, this package's own slice. The row exists so the greeting has a subject of
# its own — the PR entity is rewritten by the source on every poll, and the source is the
# single writer of github.*, so state lands here, not there.
- name: greeting
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, greet, row]
---
# An open PR in example/app gets a row. Facts only in the value: a derive merges its
# literal fields on every re-reflect, so nothing here may be row-owned state (stage lives on
# the row and is never restated by this rule).
- name: pr-noticed
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [github, pr, "*"]
      value:
        repo: example/app
        state: open
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, greet, row]
      id: $subject.id
      value:
        pr: $subject.value.number
        repo: $subject.value.repo
        title: $subject.value.title
---
# A row nobody has greeted becomes a question, and then a task somebody runs.
#
# `!not.has-path stage` is the latch (not `!not {stage: null}` — a bare null is hidden on
# read, so the durable rule would come back as `!not {}` and select everything).
- name: greet-pr
  stratum: greeting
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, greet, row, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path stage
  # THE HUMAN STEP. A firing is about one entity, so this is one ask per PR — answered with
  # `verse gate approve` (or declined), and nothing spawns until it is. The gate's id is
  # (trigger, gate, subject), so a re-fire about the same row re-asks nothing.
  #
  # A DENIED gate writes nothing, so the same row re-poses the question on the next commit
  # that touches it — the engine warns about exactly this at install. If a no should stick,
  # give the gate `onDenied: {patch: {stage: declined}}`.
  gates:
  - { name: greet, mode: ask }
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: broker
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        Post exactly one comment on pull request #$pr in the $repo repository.
        The comment body must be exactly:

            hi from verse

        For example: gh pr comment $pr --repo $repo --body "hi from verse"

        Then report it done with:

            verse task do complete <this task id> "commented" --as <you>

        If the comment cannot be posted, fail the task with the reason instead
        of completing it.
    onDone:
      patch:
        stage: commented
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        stage: failed
        err: $err
    # A cancelled greeting latches: clearing the stage would put the row back in this rule's
    # selection and spawn it again.
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        stage: declined
        err: !delete null
